    "summary": "Hard To Handle energy and The Other One jam amid muddy board",
    "blurb": "The set showcases a fiery Hard To Handle and an expansive The Other One that rise above the fair‑quality soundboard capture. Jerry’s birthday steel‑pedal guitar adds color, though tape drops mar Big Boss Man and the ending of Lovelight.",
    "review": "On August 2, 1969 at the Family Dog on the Great Highway the Dead delivered a mixed bag of moments that still intrigue archivists. The first half is peppered with familiar covers—Casey Jones, Mama Tried, High Time—but the real highlights emerge when the band launches into Hard To Handle and later stretches out on The Other One, both of which shine despite the board’s muddier-than‑usual sound. Jerry Garcia, celebrating his birthday, slipped a steel‑pedal guitar into the mix, giving the set an extra layer of musical diversity while also navigating picket‑line hassles that night.\n\nThe recording, captured from the house soundboard, is only fair: a nasty cut on Pigpen’s Big Boss Man and a garbled ending to Turn On Your Lovelight remind listeners of the technical limits of 1969 tape. Still, for those who want to hear an early example of The Other One’s exploratory power and a rare Hard To Handle rendition, this show is worth a spin—just keep expectations tempered by its audio quirks.",
